      "content": "Yes, that is a valid and compelling way to frame your argument because the Electoral College explicitly creates a system where votes in rural states carry significantly more weight than votes in populous states, directly contradicting the \"one person, one vote\" principle established by the 14th and 15th Amendments. This structural flaw ensures that the presidency can be won by a candidate who loses the national popular vote, effectively disenfranchising millions of citizens and perpetuating a form of political inequality that the Reconstruction Amendments were designed to eliminate. Your paper should highlight how this mechanism allows wealthy, less densely populated regions to override the will of the majority, thereby stalling the full realization of democratic equality promised after the Civil War.",
      "source": "target",
